<mujoco model="so101_cube_scene">
  <!-- Bare scene + a graspable cube + cameras. Milestone: sim-only object
       manipulation (docs/TELEOP_SESSION_LOG_AND_PLAN.md "Today's plan" #1).

       WHY BARE, NOT THE TABLE: the table scenes have an unresolved ~5 cm
       height mismatch (that doc, section A) -- poses the real arm reaches
       cleanly put the sim gripper below the modeled tabletop. Building the
       first working grasp on top of an unresolved geometry bug would make
       every failure ambiguous. Here the robot's base sits on the
       groundplane, which is exactly the configuration all recent
       recordings used.

       WHY THE CUBE IS WHERE IT IS -- measured, not chosen. scripts/
       analyze_workspace.py forward-kinematics a recorded teleop session
       and reports where the gripper actually went. Three independent
       sessions agree on where the operator closed the jaws:

         teleop_log_pick2.csv   x=+0.373  y=+0.027  z=0.094
         teleop_log_pick3.csv   x=+0.359  y=+0.022  (site frame)
         teleop_log_60fps.csv   x=+0.347  y=+0.020  (site frame)

       (pick2's numbers are the JAW MIDPOINT, the other two the
       gripperframe site, which sits ~2 cm higher -- so all three agree
       more closely than the raw figures suggest.)

       WHY REPLAYING A RECORDING CANNOT GRASP -- measured, not assumed.
       Those sessions were made with NO object present, so the operator
       never had anything to avoid or to open around. Two gaps matter, and
       the second one is easy to miss:

       (1) THE OPEN GAP -- how wide the jaws ever get. Tip-to-tip
       separation vs the gripper's normalised command, off the compiled
       model:

         norm    gap
          1.7   0.6 cm   fully closed -- where every session starts
         21.2   3.4 cm   <- the WIDEST any recorded session opens
         45     6.7 cm   what grasp_cube.py uses

       (2) THE APPROACH GAP. The arm does NOT approach with the gripper
       open. From teleop_log_pick2.csv, frame by frame:

         t=23.0  arrives at the target, gripper 11.19 -> gap 1.93 cm
         t=23-25 sits there, still 1.93 cm
         t=25.5  OPENS to 21.0 -> gap 3.38 cm
         t=26.0  closes
         t=26.5  fully shut, 0.59 cm

       So the jaws travel INTO the grasp position while only 1.93 cm
       apart. Any cube wide enough to be gripped is bulldozed ~3 s before
       the grasp; any cube narrow enough to survive that slips through the
       0.59 cm closed jaws. Tested across 8 sizes from 0.6 to 1.8 cm --
       none were lifted, at any size.

       This looks exactly like a contact-tuning failure and is not one. No
       friction or solref value lets a cube pass through a gap narrower
       than itself. CHECK BOTH GAPS BEFORE TUNING CONTACT.

       Hence grasp_cube.py, which opens the jaws BEFORE approaching and
       descends from above -- the order a grasp actually needs. Replay a
       recording here to watch the arm move; use grasp_cube.py to pick the
       cube up. -->

  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true" meshdir="../../../so101_assets/assets"/>

  <include file="../../../so101_assets/so101.xml"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="140" elevation="-20"/>
    <quality shadowsize="4096"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7"
             rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge"
             rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3" markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8"
             width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true"
              texrepeat="5 5" reflectance="0.2"/>
    <material name="cube_mat" rgba="0.95 0.55 0.05 1" specular="0.3" shininess="0.3"/>
    <material name="pedestal_mat" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1" specular="0.1"/>
  </asset>

  <default>
    <default class="cube_collision">
      <!-- condim=6 to match the jaw tips (so101.xml class
           "collision_gripper"). A condim=3 cube against condim=6 jaws
           gets no torsional friction, so a pinched cube spins out of the
           grasp instead of being held.

           NOTE ON WHO WINS: MuJoCo takes contact parameters from the geom
           with the higher `priority`, and so101.xml sets priority=1 on
           the jaw geoms. So the JAWS' friction (0.45, a PLA-on-PLA
           estimate) governs the grasp contact, not the numbers here --
           these apply to cube-vs-pedestal and cube-vs-floor. Tune the
           grasp by changing the jaw side, or by giving the cube a higher
           priority, not by editing friction here and expecting it to
           bite. -->
      <geom contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="6"
            friction="0.8 0.02 0.001" solref="0.01 1" solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
    <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" type="plane" material="groundplane"/>

    <!-- Overhead: looks straight down at the grasp area. The framing a
         top-down workspace camera would give. -->
    <camera name="overhead" pos="0.30 0.00 0.75" xyaxes="0 1 0 -1 0 0"/>

    <!-- Side-on close view of the grasp, for watching jaw/cube contact.
         Pulled in and raised from the first attempt (pos 0.38 -0.45 0.22),
         which framed the cube well but cut the jaws off at the top of the
         image -- the jaws are the half that matters when diagnosing why a
         grasp slipped. -->
    <camera name="grasp_view" pos="0.42 -0.34 0.30" xyaxes="1 0 0 0 0.5 0.87"/>

    <!-- Wide angle on the whole arm plus the object. -->
    <camera name="workspace" pos="0.95 -0.75 0.65" xyaxes="0.6 0.8 0 -0.45 0.34 0.82"/>

    <!-- NO PEDESTAL. An earlier version of this scene raised the cube to
         z=0.094 on a post, so that replaying teleop_log_pick2.csv would
         grasp it. That does not work, and the reason is worth keeping:

         The recorded sessions were made with NO object present, so the
         arm approaches the grasp point with the jaws nearly SHUT (1.93 cm)
         and only opens once it has arrived. Any cube big enough to be
         gripped is therefore bulldozed ~3 s before the grasp, and any cube
         small enough to survive the approach slips through the 0.59 cm
         fully-closed jaws. Measured across 8 sizes from 0.6 to 1.8 cm --
         none were lifted. There is no cube that recording can pick up.

         So the cube now sits on the FLOOR at a reachable spot, and
         grasp_cube.py drives a scripted approach-from-above that opens
         the jaws BEFORE descending. Replay a recording into this scene to
         watch the arm move; use grasp_cube.py to actually pick it up. -->

    <!-- The graspable cube. 2 cm, 3.2 g -- sized to the REAL cube on
         this bench (measured ~2 cm, 2026-09-10), not to M2's 5 cm test
         cube, so the sim object matches the physical one it stands for.
         Density 400 kg/m^3, same as M2's 5 cm / 50 g cube.

         Sits on the floor at x=0.32 -- inside the 0.08-0.38 m reach
         envelope measured by analyze_workspace.py, and at a spot where
         the arm can actually achieve a NOSE-DOWN wrist pose (verified by
         sweeping x: a top-down straddle solves cleanly at 0.32 and 0.36,
         and not at all below ~0.30, where the wrist cannot pitch over).

         Free joint, so it is fully dynamic: it can be lifted, carried,
         dropped, and knocked over. Its qpos lives at data.qpos[6:13]
         (7 values: 3 position + 4 quaternion), immediately after the
         arm's 6 joints -- which is why every script here slices the arm
         as qpos[:6] rather than assuming the whole array is the arm. -->
    <body name="cube" pos="0.3200 0.0000 0.0100">
      <freejoint name="cube_freejoint"/>
      <geom name="cube_geom" type="box" size="0.010 0.010 0.010"
            mass="0.0032" material="cube_mat" class="cube_collision"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
